    "title": "Linux System Administration and Operations — Beginner to Professional",
    "description": "A visual-first, distribution-aware Linux course spanning kernel and userspace fundamentals, terminal and file operations, administration, shell automation, networking, security, troubleshooting, developer workflows, containers, cloud operations, and professional practice.",
    "category": "Operating Systems & System Administration",
    "level": "Beginner to Professional",
    "sort_order": 66,
    "version_scope": "Linux is a kernel used with multiple userspace environments and distributions. Examples favor broadly available Linux interfaces and identify distribution-specific tools. Use the manual page and official documentation matching the installed distribution, kernel, and utility versions. Reviewed against official project/distribution references on 2026-10-20.",
    "audience": [
      "students learning Linux foundations",
      "software developers and DevOps learners",
      "system administrators and IT support professionals",
      "cloud and platform engineers",
      "cybersecurity learners working in authorized lab environments",
      "embedded/Linux systems learners choosing optional specialist topics"
    ],
    "prerequisites": [
      "Basic computer literacy and familiarity with files, folders, and paths.",
      "Programming experience is optional; C and computer architecture help with the advanced kernel and embedded tracks.",
      "A Linux-capable practice environment is recommended: disposable VM, WSL, or a spare test system. Use a VM for storage, firewall, boot, and account-changing exercises."
    ],
    "estimated_workload_hours": 180,
    "lesson_count": 257,
    "module_count": 27,
    "project_count": 13,
    "learning_outcomes": [
      "Explain the relationship among Linux kernel, userspace, distributions, hardware, and common system services.",
      "Use terminal tools to inspect and manage files, identities, permissions, packages, processes, storage, networking, and services.",
      "Write safer Bash scripts and automation with input validation, quoting, exit handling, logging, and repeatable tests.",
      "Harden and troubleshoot Linux systems using least privilege, observable evidence, supported tooling, and recovery planning.",
      "Plan a maintainable Linux deployment with verified backups, useful monitoring, documentation, and controlled change/rollback."
    ],
